yoyo find no longer prints a partial list as if it were complete. When the directory walk can't read a directory, the matches it did find still print, a note on stderr names the unreadable directories, and the command exits 1. That follows find(1); grep uses 2 because its 1 already means "no match", and a comment in the code says so. It's committed as 995d1c4f, ending Part of #982. Build, all tests, clippy and fmt pass.

Measured with the real binary (non-git tempdir, ok/target_zz.txt plus a chmod 000 locked/target_zz.txt):

| | stdout | stderr | exit |
|---|---|---|---|
| Before | 1 file matching 'target_zz': / ok/target_zz.txt | empty | 0 |
| After | same bytes | find could not read 1 directory; the list above may be incomplete: / locked (permission denied) | 1 |
| chmod 755 locked | 2 matches | empty | 0 |
| No match | unchanged | empty | 0 |

What changed
• **src/commands_search.rs:** the walk used to skip any directory it couldn't open (`Err(_) => return`). It now records the path and the reason. The output is built in `render_find_matches and is the same bytes as before. The note comes from find_unreadable_note, which cleans each path for display, cuts long ones on a character boundary and shows at most 5 directories. The REPL /find` prints the same note but has no exit code to set.
• **src/dispatch_sub.rs:** the find arm now exits with the status the handler returns, the same way the grep fix does it.
• **Side changes:** `find_files` and `walk_directory` are now test-only, since only tests still call them. I raised the `commands_search.rs` line limit in `tests/module_size.rs` from 4373 to 4460, its actual length, instead of leaving it over the limit as a warning.

Deliberately unchanged: the no-match exit code (still 0, still undecided on #982), the git-backed listing (it doesn't walk the filesystem), and the other users of the shared file list (/index, completion), which still ignore read errors. There's a comment saying /find is the only one that reports them so far.

Tests are in the new src/commands_search_find_status_tests.rs and use tempdirs only:
• An unreadable subdirectory still returns the readable match and reports the locked directory.
• A fully readable tree reports no errors, and its output matches the old output exactly.
• The note's caps and its cleaning of control characters are checked against the exact expected string.

The chmod test skips with a message if running as root. For the positive control I disabled the error recording (with a NEUTERED marker) in one atomic command: only the unreadable-directory test failed. After restoring, all 3 pass and no marker is left.

Docs and tracking: I added one sentence about this to docs/src/usage/commands.md and an entry to ARCHITECTURE.md. I also updated #982's body in place: find with an unreadable directory (995d1c4f) is now under "Already fixed", along with this morning's grep fix (27c46a29, 9fdb7650), which the body didn't list yet.
